6. INTERACTIVE ANIMATION SYSTEM

Motion must be a core part of the experience, not a decorative afterthought.

Create a cohesive motion system with the following interactions.

Global animations

Smooth page transitions where supported by the framework.

Refined navigation-menu transitions.

Animated mobile navigation drawers.

Button hover and press feedback.

Smooth scrolling for in-page links.

Subtle cursor interactions only when they add real value.

Consistent entrance and exit transitions.

Motion that adapts to screen size and input method.

Hero animations

Split-text or word-by-word headline reveals.

Masked image reveals.

Animated supporting elements.

Subtle parallax or scroll-linked movement.

Interactive visual elements, product mockups, or demonstrations.

Smooth CTA hover animations.

Keep hero animations fast, legible, and conversion-focused.

Scroll-based animation

Fade and reveal content as it enters the viewport.

Use staggered reveals for related elements.

Introduce restrained parallax for visual depth.

Create sticky storytelling sections when appropriate.

Use scroll progress indicators for long articles where useful.

Animate counters only for verified numerical claims.

Use pinned sections only when they improve the narrative.

Interactive components

Tabbed content panels.

Accordions and expandable FAQ sections.

Filterable portfolio grids.

Search overlays.

Pricing toggles.

Interactive service cards.

Before-and-after comparisons where relevant.

Image galleries and carousels with accessible controls.

Animated dropdown navigation.

Interactive product previews or feature demonstrations.

All interactive elements must work with mouse, keyboard, and touch input as applicable.

Motion engineering rules

Prefer CSS transitions and transforms for straightforward interactions.

Use a suitable animation library such as Motion for React or GSAP when the existing stack and complexity justify it.

Avoid unnecessary animation dependencies.

Animate opacity and transforms where possible rather than repeatedly triggering expensive layout changes.

Respect prefers-reduced-motion.

Avoid hijacking normal scrolling.

Do not block content behind long intro animations.

Avoid excessive bouncing, spinning, flashing, and distracting effects.

Ensure all essential content remains available when animations are disabled.

The finished experience should feel fluid, refined, and purposeful rather than over-animated.

7. FULL CMS ARCHITECTURE

The website must have a real content management system. A static collection of hardcoded pages is not sufficient.

Choose a suitable CMS based on the environment, hosting, and maintenance requirements. Examples include:

Sanity or Contentful for a hosted headless CMS.

Strapi for a self-hosted headless CMS.

WordPress for a traditional or headless CMS.

An existing supported CMS if one is already configured.

A properly implemented database-backed admin system when a custom CMS is necessary.

For an AI coding environment, prefer the most practical supported integration and explain any required external credentials or configuration.

