11. ACCESSIBILITY

Follow WCAG 2.2 AA as the design and implementation target.

Include:

Semantic landmarks and accessible markup.

Keyboard-accessible navigation and controls.

Visible focus states.

Correct labeling and instructions for forms.

Accessible validation and error messages.

Sufficient color contrast.

Appropriate text alternatives for meaningful images.

Correct dialog focus management.

Accessible accordion and tab behavior.

Reduced-motion support.

No reliance on color or animation alone to communicate essential information.

Use ARIA only where needed and implement correct keyboard behavior for custom interactive controls.

12. PERFORMANCE AND TECHNICAL QUALITY

Optimize for a fast, stable user experience.

Requirements:

Use optimized and appropriately sized images.

Lazy-load below-the-fold media where suitable.

Avoid lazy-loading the primary above-the-fold image when it would delay rendering.

Reserve image and media dimensions to reduce layout shifts.

Keep JavaScript bundles and third-party dependencies under control.

Minimize unnecessary client-side rendering.

Cache content safely where appropriate.

Use appropriate server-side rendering or static generation when supported.

Revalidate or refresh CMS-driven content after publication as necessary.

Avoid duplicate network requests.

Use robust form validation and secure server-side checks.

Handle CMS connection failures gracefully.

Avoid console errors, broken assets, missing routes, and dead links.

Target strong Core Web Vitals and an excellent Lighthouse experience without sacrificing essential functionality.

