14. DEVELOPMENT WORKFLOW

Follow this order of execution.

Phase 1 — Discovery

Inspect the existing repository, dependencies, assets, routes, and backend.

Identify the current framework and hosting constraints.

Review supplied brand material and content.

Determine the best CMS integration.

Establish the sitemap, content schemas, design system, and implementation plan.

Phase 2 — Foundation

Set up global styles and design tokens.

Build the responsive layout and navigation.

Configure routing and reusable components.

Connect the CMS and data layer.

Set up content schemas, authentication, and permissions as needed.

Phase 3 — Complete pages

Implement every required page and detail-page template.

Populate the CMS with realistic, editable sample content.

Connect each page to the correct content source.

Build the mobile layouts and global footer.

Implement real interactions and functional forms.

Phase 4 — Animation

Add the reusable motion system.

Implement scroll reveals, hover states, transitions, and interactive sections.

Add reduced-motion handling.

Test keyboard and touch behavior.

Phase 5 — CMS validation

Create and edit content through the CMS.

Verify that published changes appear on the website.

Test draft and published visibility.

Test media uploads and content relationships.

Test missing slugs and empty collections.

Verify permissions and server-side data protection.

Phase 6 — Quality assurance

Test every route.

Test all navigation, buttons, links, forms, filters, and toggles.

Test representative mobile, tablet, and desktop sizes.

Test loading, empty, error, and success states.

Check accessibility, SEO, performance, and console errors.

Fix issues before declaring the site complete.

Phase 7 — Handover

Document the route structure.

Explain how to access the CMS.

Explain how to edit and publish content.

Provide environment-variable instructions.

Document build, deployment, and integration requirements.

Identify any functionality that remains unconfigured or unverified.

15. NON-NEGOTIABLE RULES

Build the complete multi-page website, not just the homepage.

Implement every route in the approved sitemap.

Do not use placeholder links or dead-end buttons.

Do not hardcode CMS-managed content into page templates.

Do not create a fake CMS that cannot persist changes.

Do not fabricate client logos, testimonials, business statistics, or success metrics.

Do not invent API credentials or imply that an external service is connected when it is not.

Do not sacrifice mobile usability for desktop animations.

Do not add motion that distracts from reading or conversion.

Do not rewrite or destroy working parts of an existing project unnecessarily.

Do not stop after generating mockups or a visual prototype.

Do not mark unfinished pages, forms, or integrations as complete.

Reuse components and templates wherever sensible.

Make implementation decisions independently where requirements are sufficiently clear.

Fix errors discovered during testing rather than merely listing them in the final report.

16. ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA

The project is complete only when:

Every required page has a working route.

The header, navigation, footer, and internal links work.

CMS-driven pages render the correct dynamic content.

An authorized content editor can make and publish supported changes.

Content persists through the configured CMS or database.

Blog, service, case study, and other required detail pages use reusable templates.

Interactive elements have functioning behavior.

Forms have real submission handling or are clearly marked as awaiting integration configuration.

Animations work without preventing normal access to the content.

Responsive layouts have been tested at representative viewport sizes.

Accessibility and reduced-motion behaviors have been checked.

Technical SEO and metadata are implemented.

No known critical routing, content, security, or runtime errors remain.

Deployment and CMS setup instructions are documented.

